-## Subtopics
-
-### [Smoothness Splines](9886-smoothness-splines.md)
-Smoothness Splines (SS) is a scattered data approximation method for recovering volume rendered images from sparse pixel measurements by finding a smooth solution in a shift-invariant spline space. It minimizes a least-squares norm that penalizes non-smooth solutions using the second-order Beppo-Levi seminorm, distinguishing it from compressed sensing methods that rely on sparsity in wavelet or gradient domains. SS is more resilient to missing information and computationally less expensive than compressed sensing pursuit strategies.
-
-## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of splats and pixels covered by their footprints [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of voxels [G].
-*   **Strengths:** Improved accuracy under perspective projection and compact support allows for efficient culling [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires accurate covariance transformation for perspective views and standard spline parameter tuning [G].
-
-## Background
-Quadratic Spline Splatting was proposed by Max and Crawfis & Max as an alternative to Gaussian reconstruction kernels [S1, unofficial]. It was introduced to improve rendering accuracy, particularly when using perspective projection [S1, unofficial].
-
